  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "UC Berkeley law professor Catherine Crump argues that vandalism of Flock ALPR cameras reflects public frustration with an unregulated nationwide surveillance apparatus built without democratic mandate. She catalogs abuses \u2014 LAPD's lack of policy, ICE data sharing by California police, officer misuse \u2014 and calls on Congress to legislate retention limits, warrant requirements, and audit obligations.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The American people did not ask for a sprawling nationwide surveillance apparatus operated in secret and subject to few rules.",
      "paragraph_idx": 0
    },
    {
      "quote": "The company estimates 5,000 law enforcement agencies use its products \u2014 although the Los Angeles Police Department no longer numbers among them, having stopped working with Flock in July because of privacy concerns.",
      "paragraph_idx": 0
    },
    {
      "quote": "In 2020, a California State Auditor report found that of the 320 million images the Los Angeles Police Department had stored, 99.9% were of vehicles that were not on a hot list.",
      "paragraph_idx": 0
    },
    {
      "quote": "Flock, clearly feeling the pressure, has thrown privacy advocates a bone by cutting its default storage period from 30 days to seven",
      "paragraph_idx": 0
    }
  ],
